ROAST.PET FRONTIER STACK SCANNER

1 USEFUL FIND

GPT Image 2.5 Flare / Sunburst on fal =E2=80=94 TEST NOW for canonical card
generation + iterative pet edits
Source: https://fal.ai/learn/devs/gpt-image-2-5-vs-gpt-image-2 and
https://fal.ai/learn/tools/how-to-use-gpt-image-2-5
Release/current fal availability: documented Sep 13=E2=80=9314, 2026.

WHAT CHANGED
GPT Image 2.5 is now exposed on fal in two variants, Flare and Sunburst,
each with text-to-image and edit endpoints. Both add xhigh/max quality
tiers, support output up to a 3840px long edge, masks for editing, and
fal=E2=80=99s testing reports emphasize better survival of fine structure a=
cross
repeated edit rounds. Flare is speed-oriented; Sunburst spends more
inference on structural fidelity. fal lists output pricing beginning at
$0.00402/image, varying by quality.

WHY ROAST.PET CARES
This is a direct candidate for the canonical-card stage, where we need to
preserve the uploaded pet while repeatedly changing
podium/background/layout/copy. The edit path is more important than raw
text-to-image quality: if pet markings and facial geometry survive
successive masked edits, we can keep a single approved PetAsset and
generate card variants without regenerating the animal.

INPUT/OUTPUT
Text-to-image: prompt -> image. Edit: image_urls + optional mask_url +
prompt -> edited image. Up to 3840px long edge; quality ladder through max.

API/INSTALL
Hosted fal endpoints; no local install required. No self-hosted weights
established from these sources.

COST
fal reports per-output billing beginning around $0.00402; exact production
cost depends on quality/size. Benchmark actual canonical-card settings
rather than budgeting from the floor.

COMMERCIAL / PRIVACY
Hosted proprietary model; verify endpoint commercial terms before
production. Customer pet imagery is uploaded to fal/model infrastructure,
so retain local preprocessing and send only normalized PetAssets rather
than the entire customer upload bundle. No self-hosting route established.

QUALITY / LATENCY CONSTRAINTS
Generic image/edit benchmarks do not prove pet identity retention. Fine
fur, whiskers, eye color, asymmetric markings and ear geometry need a
dedicated benchmark. Flare should be tested for cheap iteration; Sunburst
only when the extra structural fidelity measurably improves pass rate.

EXACT USE CASE
customer upload -> local PetPrep -> approved PetAsset -> GPT Image 2.5 edit
-> canonical ROAST podium/card composition -> PetIdentityQC ->
typography/layout overlay -> print/AR asset

SMALLEST TEST
Use the fixed 20-pet hard corpus. For each pet: create one approved
normalized source, then perform 5 sequential edits: podium/background,
costume/prop, lighting, crop/composition, final visual cleanup. Compare
Flare vs Sunburst vs current image editor. Score marking preservation,
eye/ear geometry, whiskers/fur edges, text/layout controllability,
usable-card %, latency and cost. Also test masked background-only edits to
establish whether identity can be effectively locked.

DECISION: TEST NOW. Adopt only if identity-preserving edit pass rate beats
the current canonical-card route.

TOP 5 STACK CHANGES TO TEST NOW

   1. GPT Image 2.5 Flare vs Sunburst for canonical card + iterative masked
   edits.
   2. H3 Max / H3 Max Turbo / PixVerse V6 / Wan hard-pet video benchmark.
   3. Qwen3.8-Omni semantic audiovisual ProductionQC.
   4. Local PetPrep -> deterministic identity/edge QC -> paid generation
   fallback.
   5. N-way TTS -> STT integrity -> ComedyRanker -> animate only winner ->
   AV-QC -> enhance accepted output.

CHEAPEST MEANINGFUL CAPABILITY IMPROVEMENT
GPT Image 2.5=E2=80=99s low-end fal pricing (~$0.00402/output) makes large
canonical-card edit sweeps cheap enough to benchmark aggressively, but
local PetPrep/deterministic QC remains the lowest marginal-cost production
improvement.

BIGGEST CAPABILITY SHOCK THIS HOUR
Not a new video model. High-resolution iterative image editing is becoming
cheap enough that Roast.pet can treat canonical card creation as a
search/QC problem: generate many constrained variants while locking the pet
asset, then promote only approved frames into expensive animation.

CONCRETE STACK SIMPLIFICATION
Separate identity from design. Keep one immutable normalized PetAsset, use
masked image editing for scene/card variation, and render deterministic
typography/QR after generation. Do not ask an image model to regenerate the
pet and typography together on every iteration.

Fresh searches across GitHub, Hugging Face, arXiv/labs, fal/Replicate,
voice/TTS, segmentation, WebXR, ecommerce/POD and orchestration produced no
other sufficiently fresh, verified Roast.pet-specific delta worth padding
this run with.

